# The 2-second timeout in a test was smaller than this host's shell startup (2026-10-04)

`test_output_preserved_on_timeout` had been going red inside the full suite and green on its
own for as long as anyone had noticed. It is a test of shipped behaviour: a command that
produces output and then hangs must report the output it produced before its deadline. The
deadline branch of the actor was the suspect, and it turned out to be innocent. What was
wrong was the number 2 in the test.

== 0. What it said

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib

    test computer::local::terminal::tests::test_output_preserved_on_timeout ... FAILED
    Timed-out output should contain 'before_timeout', got: ""

`timed_out` was true and the output was empty, which reads as "the deadline threw the
buffer away". The request the test built carried a literal 2 second timeout, and the whole
of the assertion was satisfied by any output at all.

== 1. The reasonable suspicion, and the instrument that killed it

`poll_process` reads the child's pipes on every tick, and the deadline branch
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs:1866`) kills the process
group and reports what the buffer holds. The OOM and reap paths call `drain_remaining_output`
first; the deadline branch does not. So bytes sitting in the pipe at the moment of the kill
would be lost, and the test would be right to complain.

The probe prints what the actor can see, on every tick, immediately before the timeout
check, and has the child touch a marker file right after its `echo`, so "wrote late" is
distinguishable from "read wrong":

    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=<ms> buffer_len=<n> total_bytes=<n> stdout_eof=<b> stderr_eof=<b> marker_age_ms=<ms>

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/probe-timeout-output.py 9

Full-suite rounds, at 32-way parallelism, the shape repeated in every failing round:

    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1669 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1680 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1683 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1745 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1773 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1797 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1845 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1862 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1932 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1946 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=1956 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1
    TIMEOUTTICK elapsed_ms=2024 buffer_len=0 total_bytes=0 stdout_eof=false stderr_eof=false marker_age_ms=-1

Three failing rounds ended on `elapsed_ms` of 2001, 2028 and 2022, each with `buffer_len=0`,
`total_bytes=0` and `marker_age_ms=-1`. Twenty-odd polls, all of them correct about an empty
pipe, and the child's marker file never existed. There was nothing in the pipe to drain: the
shell had not run the `echo` yet when the deadline fired.

== 2. Why it had not run yet

This host's shell is zsh with a real rc file, and the shipped path replays a shell snapshot
per command. An ordinary interactive startup, measured on an idle host (load average 0.22,
28 cores):

    $ for i in 1 2 3; do /usr/bin/time -f "zsh -ic: %e s" zsh -ic 'echo M' > /dev/null; done
    zsh -ic: 0.88 s
    zsh -ic: 0.83 s
    zsh -ic: 0.88 s

    $ for i in 1 2 3; do /usr/bin/time -f "bash -lc: %e s" bash -lc 'echo M' > /dev/null; done
    bash -lc: 0.01 s
    bash -lc: 0.00 s

The `bash` line says what the rc file is worth: a login bash reaches the same byte about
ninety times faster, so the 0.9 s is this machine's zsh configuration rather than the cost of
starting a shell. Both of those are the shell reaching a prompt and writing one byte, before
the actor's own per-command work.

The same loop measured 1.32 / 1.25 / 1.45 s while the rounds of section 0 were being run, and
that is the condition the test actually failed under. It was attributed at the time to the full
suite running at 32-way parallelism; the load average of 61 was in fact mostly 52 orphaned spin
loops left on the machine by aborted diagnostic drivers, counted and cleared in
`docs/verification/shell-state-dump-grace-2026-10-04.log` section 9. The distinction matters for
the fix rather than for the conclusion: the same host at load average 0.22 still needs 0.88 s,
so a 2 second budget was never only about the machine being momentarily busy. It is a coin flip
whose odds are set by whoever else is using the machine, and the test was asserting on the
machine rather than on the code. Nothing about the deadline branch was load-sensitive; the
deadline itself was.

== 3. The fix

The budget comes from the host instead of from a literal
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs`, `test_output_preserved_on_timeout`):
one command is run to completion on the same backend and timed, and the deadline is four
times that measurement, clamped to 2 to 30 seconds. The command under test is unchanged
(`echo before_timeout; sleep 60`), the assertion is unchanged, and the failure message now
carries the measured startup so the next reader can see what the host said:

    let mut request = make_request("echo before_timeout; sleep 60");
    request.timeout = (startup * 4).clamp(Duration::from_secs(2), Duration::from_secs(30));

A slow machine gets a slow deadline. The one-time snapshot and login-environment capture are
absorbed by a warm-up command first, so what is measured is the cost of starting one shell,
not the cost of the first command ever.

== 4. What this does not settle

The asymmetry from section 1 is real and untouched: the deadline branch reports the buffer
without a final drain, while the OOM and reap paths drain first. No observed failure had
bytes to lose, so no product change was made on the strength of a hypothesis. It is recorded
in `docs/ci-test-debt.md` so the next person does not have to re-derive it, and a command
that writes and is killed in the same tick remains the shape that would show it.

== 5. Verification

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib -- computer::local::terminal::tests::test_output_preserved_on_timeout
    test computer::local::terminal::tests::test_output_preserved_on_timeout ... ok
    test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3214 filtered out; finished in 5.00s

Three rounds back to back on the idle host: 5.00 s, 4.90 s, 5.10 s. An earlier recording of this
same line said 11.81 s, which is what the test costs on the machine described in section 2; the
test measures the host three times over (warm-up, calibration, then the command cut at its
deadline), so host slowness arrives multiplied here as well.

What the test derives for itself was printed once, by an ephemeral `eprintln!` placed in the test
body after the measurement, run under `--nocapture`, with the file restored byte-identically
afterwards (`{SCRATCH}/probe-budget.py` prints the value and the restore check):

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib -- --nocapture \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_output_preserved_on_timeout
    TIMEOUTBUDGET startup=704.369614ms deadline=2.817478456s

On this host the deadline lands at 2.8 s, above the 2 s floor, so the clamp is not what is holding
the test up; four times 0.7 s is the budget. The calibration reading is smaller than the bare
`zsh -ic` of section 2 because it goes through the shipped backend rather than a login shell
started from a terminal.

and it is green inside the full-suite rounds recorded in
`docs/verification/shell-state-dump-grace-2026-10-04.log` section 10, which is where it used
to fail; the multiplier was lowered from 6 to 4 after the first measurement, because 6 times
a slow host's startup would have made the test the slowest thing in the file for no added
coverage.
